{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches to the target concept of nodular goiter via either multinodular/nontoxic multinodular goiter (PGS001814, PGS002022) or benign nodular goiter (PGS005261–PGS005263). Two additional models (PGS005273–PGS005274) are case-control contrasts of thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter and are therefore not clean direct models of nodular goiter itself for this target.

Between the clean nodular-goiter endpoints, the 2026 multi-cohort thyroid disease study family (PGS005261–PGS005263; medRxiv) provides the strongest disease-focused context and large validation size (n=94,651), which the domain policy favors over UKB portability/framework-style scores (PGS001814/PGS002022; 2022 portability paper) whose only reported PRS-comparable signal is weak partial correlation (partial-r ~0.024–0.028). Within the benign nodular goiter family, PGS005262 has the best available reported discrimination in this context (full_model_auc 0.5933 vs 0.5876 for PGS005263 and 0.5854 for PGS005261) and also shows the strongest per-SD effect sizes (OR 1.41, β 0.34), making it the best-supported pick among direct matches.

Limitations lowering the outcome to sub-optimal: for the selected 2026 models the covariates are listed as "Unknown" and only full-model AUROC is provided (no clearly PRS-only AUROC/R2), so comparability of the performance metric is uncertain; additionally, the goiter subtype differs (benign nodular vs nontoxic multinodular), and the portability models have different phenotype framing despite having more explicit covariates."}

